Subject: [PATCH] Keep the core clock when the frontend perf clock does not tick

Kodi's game.libretro answers RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_GET_PERF_INTERFACE with a
callback that always returns 0. DOSBox Pure hands that callback to time_cb and
derives DBP_GetTicks() from it, so the tick counter stays at 0 for the whole
session under Kodi.

Every tick based timeout of the core depends on it: the start menu timer
(dosbox_pure_menu_time), the "exit emulator after the auto started game ends"
auto shutdown, and the 500 ms guard that tells a real game run from a crashed
one. With the frozen counter the guard never passes, so a game that ends drops
back into the start menu instead of closing the player and returning to Kodi.

Probe the callback once and keep the core's own monotonic clock when the
frontend one returns 0. A frontend with a working perf clock is unaffected.

diff --git a/dosbox_pure_libretro.cpp b/dosbox_pure_libretro.cpp
index ea24d45..6d496ee 100644
--- a/dosbox_pure_libretro.cpp
+++ b/dosbox_pure_libretro.cpp
@@ -3063,8 +3063,13 @@ void retro_init(void) //#3
 	void DBP_Network_SetCallbacks(retro_environment_t envcb);
 	DBP_Network_SetCallbacks(environ_cb);
 
+	// Some frontends answer the perf interface with a stub that always returns 0
+	// (Kodi's game.libretro does). Accepting it freezes DBP_GetTicks(), and with it
+	// every tick based timeout: the start menu timer, the auto shutdown after a game
+	// and the menu input debounce. Probe the callback and keep the core's own
+	// monotonic clock when the frontend one does not tick.
 	struct retro_perf_callback perf;
-	if (environ_cb(RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_GET_PERF_INTERFACE, &perf) && perf.get_time_usec) time_cb = perf.get_time_usec;
+	if (environ_cb(RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_GET_PERF_INTERFACE, &perf) && perf.get_time_usec && perf.get_time_usec()) time_cb = perf.get_time_usec;
 
 	// Set default port modes
 	dbp_port_mode[0] = dbp_port_mode[1] = dbp_port_mode[2] = dbp_port_mode[3] = DBP_PadMapping::MODE_MAPPER;
